(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-07"] = {"chem-11-07-m01":{"idea":"Adsorption is a surface effect, not a bulk effect.","steps":["In adsorption, molecules stick to the surface of a solid or liquid.","The substance taken up is the adsorbate. The solid is the adsorbent.","If the molecules go into the whole body, it is called absorption."],"others":"Reacting with the whole volume is not adsorption. Entering the whole body is absorption. Separating from a surface is the opposite process, called desorption.","trap":"Adsorption = surface. Absorption = whole body.","answer":"stick to the surface of a solid or a liquid"},"chem-11-07-m02":{"idea":"Telling absorption from adsorption by where the substance ends up.","steps":["A sponge takes water through its whole body, so this is absorption.","Charcoal holds gas only on its surface, so this is adsorption.","So the pair is absorption, then adsorption."],"others":"Calling both adsorption or both absorption ignores the difference. Adsorption for the sponge and absorption for charcoal reverses the pair.","answer":"absorption and adsorption"},"chem-11-07-m03":{"idea":"Physical adsorption uses the same weak forces that act between molecules.","steps":["Physisorption is caused by weak van der Waals forces.","Its heat of adsorption is low, about 20 to 40 kJ/mol.","It is reversible and not specific, and it can form many layers."],"others":"Nuclear forces and ionic bonds play no role. Strong chemical bonds belong to chemisorption, not physical adsorption.","answer":"weak van der Waals forces between the gas and the surface"},"chem-11-07-m04":{"idea":"Chemisorption involves real chemical bonds between gas and surface.","steps":["Chemisorption needs a chemical reaction at the surface.","So it is specific: only some gases stick to some surfaces.","It stops at one layer, a monolayer.","Its heat is high, about 80 to 240 kJ/mol."],"others":"Many layers and no specificity describe physisorption. A low enthalpy with no effect from the surface is also physisorption. Reversible with van der Waals forces is also physisorption.","answer":"it is specific, and it is limited to a single layer"},"chem-11-07-m05":{"idea":"Adsorption gives out heat, so Le Chatelier's principle applies.","steps":["Physisorption is exothermic.","Raising the temperature pushes the equilibrium towards desorption.","So the amount adsorbed decreases when the temperature rises."],"others":"It does not turn into chemisorption at once. A surface effect still depends on temperature. Faster molecules escape the surface more easily, so adsorption does not increase.","trap":"Cooling helps physical adsorption.","answer":"decreases, since the adsorption is exothermic"},"chem-11-07-m06":{"idea":"Gases with strong van der Waals forces stick more strongly.","steps":["Easily liquefied gases have strong attractions between molecules.","These same forces bind them to the charcoal surface.","So NH₃, Cl₂ and SO₂ are adsorbed more than H₂, N₂ or He."],"others":"A low critical temperature or being hard to liquefy means weak forces, so less adsorption. Smallest molar mass does not decide adsorption.","answer":"is easily liquefied, like ammonia or chlorine"},"chem-11-07-m07":{"idea":"Adsorption happens on the surface, so more surface means more adsorption.","steps":["Dividing a solid into a fine powder greatly increases its surface area.","More surface gives more places for gas molecules to stick.","So more gas is adsorbed."],"others":"An oil layer blocks the surface. Heating to a high temperature before use does not help; it lowers physical adsorption. A single lump has a small surface area.","answer":"divided into a fine powder, which gives a larger surface area"},"chem-11-07-m08":{"idea":"The Freundlich isotherm links the amount adsorbed to the pressure.","steps":["x is the mass of gas adsorbed and m is the mass of adsorbent.","The equation is x/m = kP^(1/n).","k and n are constants for the system and temperature.","The log form is log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log P."],"others":"x/m = kP would be a straight proportion, which fails at high pressure. k/P^(1/n) falls with pressure. kP^n has the wrong power.","answer":"x/m = kP^(1/n)"},"chem-11-07-m09":{"idea":"In the log form log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log P, compare with y = c + mx.","steps":["The graph is a straight line of log(x/m) against log P.","The slope is 1/n and the intercept is log k.","n is greater than 1, so 1/n lies between 0 and 1."],"others":"n itself is not the slope. log k is the intercept, and it can be negative. k/n is not the slope, and it is not always 1.","answer":"1/n, and its value lies between 0 and 1"},"chem-11-07-m10":{"idea":"A gas mask must remove poisonous gases from air.","steps":["Activated charcoal has a very large surface area.","Poisonous gases stick to this surface (adsorption).","The air that passes through becomes safe to breathe."],"others":"Charcoal does not react with all gases to make solids. It does not absorb like a sponge. It does not heat the air.","answer":"adsorbs the poisonous gases on its large surface"},"chem-11-07-m11":{"idea":"Dryness is kept by removing water vapour from the air.","steps":["Silica gel is a porous solid with a huge surface area.","Water vapour sticks to this surface (adsorption).","It can be heated in an oven and used again."],"others":"It does not heat the air. It does not dissolve water. It does not react to form a new compound, or it could not be reused.","answer":"adsorbs the water vapour from the air on its surface"},"chem-11-07-m12":{"idea":"A catalyst changes speed but is left unchanged at the end.","steps":["A catalyst gives the reaction a new path with lower activation energy.","So the rate changes.","The catalyst is not consumed.","It does not change ΔH or the equilibrium constant."],"others":"A catalyst cannot start an impossible reaction, and it is not used up. It does not change the enthalpy. It does not change the position of equilibrium.","answer":"changes the rate of a reaction, and is not used up in it"},"chem-11-07-m13":{"idea":"A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally.","steps":["The equilibrium constant depends only on ΔG, which a catalyst does not change.","So the final amounts of reactants and products stay the same.","Only the time to reach equilibrium becomes shorter."],"others":"Catalysts do not change the enthalpy of reaction. They do not change the equilibrium composition, so changing both is wrong. They do change the time, so saying the time stays the same is also wrong.","answer":"the time taken to reach equilibrium, but not the equilibrium composition"},"chem-11-07-m14":{"idea":"Heterogeneous means the catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants.","steps":["Homogeneous catalysis has all substances in one phase.","In heterogeneous catalysis, the catalyst is in a different phase.","Example: solid iron with gaseous N₂ and H₂ in the Haber process."],"others":"The catalyst can be a solid, so it is not always a liquid or always a gas. The same phase as the reactants is homogeneous catalysis.","answer":"is in a phase different from that of the reactants"},"chem-11-07-m15":{"idea":"Some substances make a catalyst work better, and some make it work worse.","steps":["A promoter increases catalyst activity, for example Mo with Fe in the Haber process.","A poison reduces it, for example arsenic compounds with platinum.","So the answer is a promoter."],"others":"A poison and an inhibitor lower activity or rate. An adsorbent is a substance that holds molecules on its surface, which is not what is asked.","answer":"a promoter"},"chem-11-07-m16":{"idea":"Heterogeneous catalysis happens in steps on the surface.","steps":["Reactants diffuse to the surface.","They are adsorbed on the surface.","They react to form an intermediate and then the product.","The product is desorbed and diffuses away."],"others":"The other orders put desorption before adsorption, or the product before the intermediate. A product cannot leave the surface before it forms.","trap":"Diffuse, adsorb, react, desorb.","answer":"diffusion, adsorption, formation of an intermediate, formation of the product, desorption"},"chem-11-07-m17":{"idea":"Colloids sit between true solutions and suspensions by particle size.","steps":["True solution particles are less than about 2 nm.","Suspension particles are larger than about 1000 nm, and they settle.","Colloid particles are 2 nm to 500 nm."],"others":"0.1 nm to 2 nm is the true-solution range. Less than 0.1 nm is smaller than most atoms. More than 1000 nm is a suspension.","answer":"2 nm to 500 nm"},"chem-11-07-m18":{"idea":"Colloid particles scatter light.","steps":["A beam of light passes through a colloid.","The particles scatter the light, so the path becomes visible.","This is the Tyndall effect."],"others":"Coagulation is the settling of particles. Electrophoresis is movement in an electric field. Brownian movement is the zigzag motion of particles.","answer":"the Tyndall effect"},"chem-11-07-m19":{"idea":"Colloids have special names for their two parts.","steps":["The particles spread out form the dispersed phase.","The medium they are spread in is the dispersion medium.","For gold sol, gold is the dispersed phase and water is the medium."],"others":"Solvent and solute are terms for true solutions. The dispersed phase is the particles, not the medium. Adsorbent and adsorbate are terms for adsorption.","answer":"dispersion medium, and the particles are the dispersed phase"},"chem-11-07-m20":{"idea":"A colloid has particles of 2 nm to 500 nm spread in another phase.","steps":["Milk has tiny fat droplets spread in water.","The droplets are of colloidal size, so milk is an emulsion.","Salt water is a true solution, sand in water is a suspension, and air is a gas mixture."],"others":"Salt in water has ions smaller than 1 nm. Sand settles out. Air is a mixture of gases, a true solution.","answer":"milk, which is an emulsion of fat in water"},"chem-11-07-m21":{"idea":"Only a colloid scatters light and also stays suspended.","steps":["Starch gives a colloid in water, with particles of 2 to 500 nm.","These scatter light, so starch in water shows the Tyndall effect, and they do not settle.","Sugar gives a true solution and does not scatter light.","Sand makes a suspension and settles."],"others":"Sugar in water is a true solution. Sand in water settles. So not all three show the effect.","answer":"starch in water"},"chem-11-07-n01":{"idea":"Put the numbers into the Freundlich isotherm x/m = kP^(1/n).","steps":["k = 0.50, n = 2, so 1/n = 0.5, and P = 4.0 atm.","x/m = 0.50 × 4.0^0.5.","4.0^0.5 = 2, so x/m = 0.50 × 2 = 1.000 g per g.","Check: log(x/m) = −0.301 + 0.5 × 0.602 = 0, so x/m = 1."],"answer":"1.000 g per g"},"chem-11-07-n02":{"idea":"On a log–log graph the Freundlich line has slope 1/n.","steps":["Take the first two points: P goes from 1.0 to 4.0 atm, and x/m goes from 0.50 to 1.0.","Slope = (log 1.0 − log 0.50) ÷ (log 4.0 − log 1.0).","Slope = 0.301 ÷ 0.602 = 0.50.","So 1/n = 0.50 and n = 2.00."],"trap":"The third point agrees: 16 atm gives 2.0.","answer":"2.00"},"chem-11-07-n03":{"idea":"Cutting a solid into small pieces greatly raises its surface area.","steps":["Total area = 6V ÷ a for small cubes of side a that make up volume V.","V = 1.0 cm³ and a = 10⁻⁷ cm.","Area = 6 × 1.0 ÷ 10⁻⁷ = 6.0 × 10⁷ cm².","The original cube had only 6 cm²."],"trap":"This is why powders adsorb so much.","answer":"6.0 × 10⁷ cm²"},"chem-11-07-n04":{"idea":"Flocculation value is the millimoles of electrolyte needed per litre of sol to cause coagulation.","steps":["Millimoles of NaCl = 0.50 mL × 1.0 mmol/mL = 0.50 mmol.","Volume of sol = 10 mL = 0.010 L.","Flocculation value = 0.50 ÷ 0.010 = 50 mmol per litre.","Check: for 1 L of sol you would need 0.50 × 100 = 50 mmol."],"answer":"50 mmol per litre"},"chem-11-07-n05":{"idea":"The Arrhenius equation k = A e^(−Ea/RT) shows how activation energy changes the rate constant.","steps":["The ratio is k(cat) ÷ k = exp[(Ea − Ea′) ÷ RT], since A is unchanged.","Ea − Ea′ = 100 000 − 80 000 = 20 000 J/mol.","RT = 8.314 × 300 = 2494.2 J/mol.","Ratio = exp(20 000 ÷ 2494.2) = exp(8.019) ≈ 3037."],"trap":"Convert kJ to J before using R.","answer":"3037 times"},"chem-11-07-n06":{"idea":"Number of molecules in one layer = surface area ÷ area covered by one molecule.","steps":["Area = 1000 m². One molecule covers 0.20 nm² = 0.20 × 10⁻¹⁸ m².","Molecules = 1000 ÷ (0.20 × 10⁻¹⁸) = 5.0 × 10²¹.","Moles = 5.0 × 10²¹ ÷ 6.022 × 10²³ = 8.3 × 10⁻³ mol.","Volume = 8.3 × 10⁻³ × 22.4 L = 0.186 L = 186 mL."],"answer":"186 mL"}};
